/*  `extern additive property` states a fact; it emits no directive.

    I6 ACCUMULATES an additive property: a class default and an instance override are both
    kept. That is the point of `name` — a wooden box should match "wooden", "box" and
    "crate" — and Beguile expresses it with array<dictionaryWord>.

    It breaks only when the member's type expects a SINGLE word, which is why the compiler
    needs to know which properties accumulate. `additive` on a non-extern property emits
    `Property additive`; on an extern one it emits nothing, because I6 already declared it.

    Pins: `name` — declared `extern additive` by the core BLR, since it is additive in the I6
    compiler rather than in any library — produces no `Property additive name;` line, and the
    non-extern form still emits its directive.  */
#beguilerSettings { target = z5; informName = "none"; outputPath = "tests/output"; }

additive property rawArray<var> myHooks;          // ours; emits `Property additive myHooks;`

void main(){ bglInit(); }
